  "beruf": "kita",
  "title": "Cleaning and domestic work",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "portuguese-for-work run, 2026-11-22: written by hand for Portugal (European Portuguese, pt-PT), not yet read by a human. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon, the IMT deck or another sector are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions, contracts and rules are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Cleaning jobs in offices, holiday flats and private homes are a common first job in Portugal, and many families employ an empregada doméstica who also looks after the children. This deck has the products and tools, the jobs in a house or an office, the working day, safety with cleaning products, and talking to the children and the family you work for. Colleagues say tu; with the family, start with the third person (o senhor, a senhora, Dona Teresa) until they offer tu.",
  "introEasy": "Do you clean or look after a home in Portugal? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Empregado/a de limpeza",
    "Empregada doméstica",
    "Auxiliar de limpeza",
    "Ama",
    "Babysitter"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Ask before you use a product on a surface you don't know: «Posso usar lixívia aqui?» Marble, wooden floors and stainless steel need their own products.",
    "Never mix lixívia (bleach) with other cleaning products, especially ones with ammonia or acid: the fumes are dangerous. Open the window.",
    "Work in a private home is its own kind of contract (serviço doméstico). The family should register you with the Segurança Social; ask for a written agreement and a receipt for every payment. General orientation, not legal advice.",
    "With children, speak slowly and clearly, praise often («Muito bem!») and tell the parents at the end of the day what happened."
  ],
